The rewritten `update_environment_variables` handler applies every string-valued entry of the `variables` map straight into the live process environment:
```
if let Some(env_vars) = frame.get("variables").and_then(Value::as_object) {
    for (k, v) in env_vars {
        if let Some(val) = v.as_str() { std::env::set_var(k, val); }
    }
}
```
The 2.1.218 oracle does NOT do this. Its structuredIO processLine enforces an explicit allowlist and refuses every other key:
`UtS=new Set(["CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ACCESS_TOKEN","CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"])` and `for(let[i,s]of Object.entries(t.variables)){if(!UtS.has(i)){o.push(i);continue}process.env[i]=s,...}` … `if(o.length>0)C("[structuredIO] refused update_environment_variables for non-allowlisted keys: "+o.join(", "))`. The oracle also DROPS the entire frame (and emits a control_response error when request_id is set) if any value is non-string; the port silently skips non-string values instead. The prior main-branch code was already narrower (only `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`), so this WIP widened env-injection from one auth key to unbounded. The in-code comment ("Claude Code's structuredIO applies every entry … directly to the live process") is factually wrong about the oracle. This is both a parity regression and a security under-restriction: an SDK peer driving stream-json input can now set process env vars that the oracle deliberately blocks, and those vars are inherited by the Bash tool's `/bin/sh -c` child and by the HTTP/auth stack.
